ns different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in none.","t_rain":"Lawton has gotten drier","p_rain":"Lawton's yearly precipitation has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.53 inches per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.99 to \u22120.07), about \u22125.9 inches since 1913. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 27.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1919 (48.04 in); the driest, 2003 (14.25 in). Lawton almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.6 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 10 daily record highs, 3 daily record lows"}}